============================================================================================================
PART 4 -- NEAR MISSES.  Where a repair is most likely to be possible.
============================================================================================================

  UNIT WARNING: G6's margin is log10(sigma), not a dex ratio of a physical quantity, and
  G5/G9's are tuning sizes.  Summing them is a sort key, nothing more; the per-gate margins
  printed under each entry are the numbers to quote.
  Ranking rule: an architecture is a near-miss only if EVERY gate it fails is 'continuous'
  or 'tuning' -- i.e. the failure has a size that a repair would have to cover.  Structural
  failures (a ghost, a wrong-sign operator, a wrong-ordered filter) are not near-misses at
  any margin, because no continuous knob reaches them.  Margins are summed in dex; that sum
  is a RANKING heuristic across incommensurable quantities, not a likelihood.

  96 of 4,200 architectures fail ONLY on continuous/tuning gates.

  Grouped by (failing-gate set, margin) -- architectures differing only in a gate-blind axis
